<Doc title="The 7-Day Edge Reset" eyebrow="Your free guide" lede="One small change a day. Do the day, tick it off, and don't read ahead." noindex>
  <div class="callout"><p>This guide is general information, not medical advice. If you have a health condition or haven't exercised in a long time, check with your doctor before you start.</p></div>

  <h2>How this works</h2>
  <p>Each day has one action. Most take ten to thirty minutes. The point is not to transform your life in a week. It is to prove to yourself that small changes are doable, and to leave you with a baseline.</p>
  <p>You'll need a notebook and a pen.</p>

  <h2>Day 1. Mind: empty your head</h2>
  <p>Sit somewhere quiet for ten minutes with your notebook. Write down everything that is weighing on you: work, money, health, family, the thing you keep putting off. Don't solve anything. Just get it onto paper.</p>
  <p>Then circle the one item that drains you most. That's all for today.</p>
  <p><strong>Why:</strong> stress you haven't named runs in the background all day. A list is easier to carry than a fog.</p>

  <h2>Day 2. Measure: take your starting numbers</h2>
  <p>Write down today's date and record:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Your weight, first thing in the morning</li>
    <li>Your waist, measured at the navel</li>
    <li>Your blood pressure, if you have a monitor or can use one at a pharmacy</li>
    <li>How many hours you slept last night</li>
    <li>Your energy today, out of 10</li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong>Why:</strong> in three months you'll want proof of how far you've come.</p>

  <h2>Day 3. Strength: fifteen minutes</h2>
  <p>Do three rounds, resting a minute between rounds:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>8 squats to a chair: sit down slowly, stand up</li>
    <li>8 push-ups, with your hands on a counter or bench if the floor is too hard</li>
    <li>8 hip hinges: hands on hips, push your hips back, stand tall</li>
    <li>A 20-second plank, from your knees if you need to</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Write down how many rounds you finished.</p>
  <p><strong>Why:</strong> the first session only has to be easy enough that you'll do a second.</p>

  <h2>Day 4. Fuel: protein at every meal</h2>
  <p>Change one thing about your food today: include a palm-sized serving of protein at breakfast, lunch and dinner. Eggs, Greek yogurt, chicken, fish, beef, tofu or beans all count.</p>
  <p>Don't cut anything out. Just add the protein.</p>
  <p><strong>Why:</strong> protein keeps you full and protects muscle, and adding is easier than taking away.</p>

  <h2>Day 5. Recovery: protect the last hour</h2>
  <p>Choose a bedtime that gives you at least seven and a half hours before your alarm. An hour before it, put your phone on charge in another room. Read, stretch, shower or talk to someone.</p>
  <p><strong>Why:</strong> everything else on this list works better on a good night's sleep.</p>

  <h2>Day 6. Strength: beat Day 3</h2>
  <p>Repeat the Day 3 session and add one rep to each exercise, or one extra round. Write it down.</p>
  <p><strong>Why:</strong> doing slightly more than last time is the entire secret of getting stronger.</p>

  <h2>Day 7. Plan: choose your lever</h2>
  <p>Look back at your week and answer three questions in your notebook:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Which day made the biggest difference to how I felt?</li>
    <li>Which was hardest, and why?</li>
    <li>Which one lever will I work on for the next ninety days?</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Then put three strength sessions and a fixed bedtime in your calendar for next week.</p>

  <h2>What next</h2>
  <p>Keep the routine you've started: three short strength sessions, protein at each meal, a fixed bedtime and ten quiet minutes. That alone puts you ahead of where you were a week ago.</p>
  <p>When you're ready for more, read <a href="/articles/strength-training-after-40/">the minimum effective strength plan</a>, and watch your inbox. I send one email a week with the next step.</p>
  <p>Todd</p>
